

**Example 1.** Lemmiscatic Case

(a) Given _z=x+iy_ in the Fundamental Triangle, find \({\cal D}({\cal D}^{\prime},\xi,\sigma)\) more accurately than can be done with the maps.

\(\sigma\)--Use Maclaurin series throughout the Fundamental Triangle. Five terms give at least six significant figures, six terms at least ten. \({\cal D}\), \(\xi\)--Use Laurent's series directly "near" 0, (if |_z_|<1, four terms give at least eight significant figures for \({\cal D}\), nine for \(\xi\); five terms at least ten significant figures for \({\cal D}\), eleven for \(\xi\)). Use Taylor's series directly "near" \(z_{6}\). Elsewhere (unless approximately seven or eight significant figures are insufficient) use economized polynomials to obtain \({\cal D}(x)\), \({\cal D}^{\prime}(x)\) and/or \(\xi(x)\) as appropriate. To get \({\cal D}(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\), \({\cal D}^{\prime}(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\) and/or \(\xi(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\), use Laurent's series for "small" \(y\), otherwise use economized polynomials to compute \({\cal D}(y)\), \({\cal D}^{\prime}(y)\) and/or \(\xi(y)\), then use complex multiplication to obtain \({\cal D}(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\), \({\cal D}^{\prime}(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\) and/or \(\xi(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\). Finally, use appropriate addition formula to get \({\cal D}(z)\) and/or \(\xi(z)\).

\({\cal D}^{\prime}\)--Use Laurent's series directly "near" 0 (if |_z_|<1, four terms give at least six significant figures, five terms at least eight significant figures). Elsewhere, either use economized polynomials and addition formula as for \({\cal D}\) and \(\xi\), or get \({\cal D}^{\prime}\) means 4 \({\cal D}^{\prime}\) and extract appropriate square root (\({\cal J}{\cal D}^{\prime}\geq 0\)).

(b) Given \({\cal D}({\cal D}^{\prime},\xi,\sigma)\) corresponding to a point in the Fundamental Triangle, compute \(z\) more accurately than can be done with the maps. Only a few significant figures are obtainable from the use of any of the given (truncated) reversed series, except in a small neighborhood of the center of the series. For greater accuracy, use inverse interpolation procedures.

**Example 2.** Equianharmonic Case

(a) Given _z=x+iy_ in the Fundamental Triangle, find \({\cal D}({\cal D}^{\prime},\xi,\sigma)\) more accurately than can be done with the maps.

\(\sigma\)--Use Maclaurin series throughout the Fundamental Triangle. Four terms give at least eleven significant figures, five terms at least twenty one.

\({\cal D}\),\(\xi\)--Use Laurent's series directly "near" 0 (if |_z_|<1, four terms give at least 10S for \({\cal D}\), 11S for \(\xi\); five terms at least 13S for \({\cal D}\), 14S for \(\xi\)). Elsewhere (unless approximately seven or eight significant figures are insufficient) use economized polynomials to obtain \({\cal D}(x)\), \({\cal D}^{\prime}(x)\) and/or \(\xi(x)\), as appropriate. To get \({\cal D}(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\), \({\cal D}^{\prime}(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\) and/or \(\xi(i\mskip-2.0mu y)\), use Laurent's series. Then use appropriate addition formula to get \({\cal D}(z)\) and/or \(\xi(z)\).

